PAGES["testament"] = dict(people=["william-salesbury", "richard-davies"],
 links=[("person-william-salesbury.html", "William Salesbury", "William Salesbury"), ("person-richard-davies.html", "Richard Davies", "Richard Davies"), ("book-bible.html", "William Morgan’s Bible", "Beibl William Morgan")],
 pEn=[
  "|| Printed | 1567, in London",
  "|| Translated by | William Salesbury, Bishop Richard Davies and Thomas Huet",
  "|| Made possible by | the Act of Parliament of 1563 for a Welsh Bible",
  "|| Famous for | the first New Testament and Prayer Book in Welsh",
  "## A law for the Welsh Bible",
  "In 1563 an Act of Parliament ordered that the Bible and the Book of Common Prayer be translated into Welsh, and placed in every church in Wales beside the English, by St David’s Day 1567. It was a great victory for the Welsh language, won by Welshmen who saw that the faith of their people must be in their own tongue.",
  "## The translators",
  "The work fell to William Salesbury of Llansannan, the most learned Welshman of his age, who had already printed a Welsh dictionary and a book of Welsh proverbs. He worked with Richard Davies, Bishop of St Davids, in the bishop’s palace at Abergwili, near Carmarthen. Salesbury translated most of the New Testament. Davies translated five of the letters, and Thomas Huet, precentor of St Davids, translated the Book of Revelation. The Prayer Book, Salesbury’s work, came out the same year.",
  "Salesbury wrote his Welsh to show its ancient dignity, spelling many words to show their roots in Latin, the language of the Britons’ Roman past. Bishop Davies wrote a great preface, the Epistol at y Cembru, the Letter to the Welsh, in which he told them that their faith was older than the English church, brought to the Britons in the days of the apostles, and that in reading the scriptures in Welsh they were coming home to the faith of their forefathers.",
  "## What followed",
  "For the first time the people of Wales could hear the whole New Testament read in their own language. Twenty-one years later, William Morgan, who had learned from Salesbury’s work, gave them the whole Bible.",
 ],
 pCy=[
  "|| Argraffwyd | 1567, yn Llundain",
  "|| Cyfieithwyd gan | William Salesbury, yr Esgob Richard Davies a Thomas Huet",
  "|| Yn bosibl drwy | Ddeddf Seneddol 1563 dros Feibl Cymraeg",
  "|| Enwog am | y Testament Newydd a’r Llyfr Gweddi cyntaf yn Gymraeg",
  "## Deddf dros y Beibl Cymraeg",
  "Yn 1563 gorchmynnodd Deddf Seneddol fod y Beibl a’r Llyfr Gweddi Gyffredin yn cael eu cyfieithu i’r Gymraeg, a’u gosod ym mhob eglwys yng Nghymru wrth ochr y Saesneg, erbyn Gŵyl Ddewi 1567. Roedd yn fuddugoliaeth fawr i’r iaith Gymraeg, a enillwyd gan Gymry a welai fod yn rhaid i ffydd eu pobl fod yn eu hiaith eu hunain.",
  "## Y cyfieithwyr",
  "Syrthiodd y gwaith ar William Salesbury o Lansannan, y Cymro mwyaf dysgedig yn ei oes, a oedd eisoes wedi argraffu geiriadur Cymraeg a llyfr o ddiarhebion Cymraeg. Gweithiodd gyda Richard Davies, Esgob Tyddewi, ym mhlas yr esgob yn Abergwili, ger Caerfyrddin. Cyfieithodd Salesbury y rhan fwyaf o’r Testament Newydd. Cyfieithodd Davies bump o’r epistolau, a chyfieithodd Thomas Huet, cantor Tyddewi, Lyfr y Datguddiad. Daeth y Llyfr Gweddi, gwaith Salesbury, allan yr un flwyddyn.",
  "Ysgrifennodd Salesbury ei Gymraeg i ddangos ei hurddas hynafol, gan sillafu llawer gair i ddangos ei wreiddiau yn y Lladin, iaith gorffennol Rhufeinig y Brythoniaid. Ysgrifennodd yr Esgob Davies ragymadrodd mawr, yr Epistol at y Cembru, lle dywedodd wrthynt fod eu ffydd yn hŷn nag eglwys Lloegr, wedi’i dwyn at y Brythoniaid yn nyddiau’r apostolion, ac wrth ddarllen yr ysgrythurau yn Gymraeg eu bod yn dod adref at ffydd eu cyndadau.",
  "## Yr hyn a ddilynodd",
  "Am y tro cyntaf gallai pobl Cymru glywed y Testament Newydd cyfan yn cael ei ddarllen yn eu hiaith eu hunain. Un mlynedd ar hugain yn ddiweddarach, rhoddodd William Morgan, a ddysgodd o waith Salesbury, y Beibl cyfan iddynt.",
 ])

PAGES["bible"] = dict(people=["william-morgan", "edmwnd-prys", "john-davies-mallwyd", "william-salesbury"],
 links=[("person-william-morgan.html", "William Morgan", "William Morgan"), ("place-llanrhaeadr.html", "Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant", "Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant"), ("book-testament.html", "The New Testament of 1567", "Testament Newydd 1567")],
 pEn=[
  "|| Printed | 1588, in London, by the deputies of Christopher Barker",
  "|| Translated by | William Morgan, vicar of Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant",
  "|| From | the Hebrew and the Greek",
  "|| Placed | in every parish church in Wales",
  "## The book that saved a language",
  "Y Beibl Cyssegr-lan, the Holy Bible, came out in 1588, the year of the Spanish Armada. It was the work of William Morgan, born at Tŷ Mawr Wybrnant, in the hills above Penmachno, the son of a tenant farmer, who studied at Cambridge and became vicar of Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant in the Berwyn hills. There, in a country parish, he translated the whole Bible into Welsh from the Hebrew and the Greek, and revised Salesbury’s New Testament as well.",
  "His Welsh was the Welsh of the poets: rich, dignified and clear, the language of the bardic schools that had kept the tongue pure for a thousand years. When his Bible was placed in every parish church, that language was heard every Sunday in every corner of Wales. It gave the Welsh a standard of written Welsh that all could share, north and south, and it is the reason, many believe, that Welsh is spoken today when so many other languages of these islands have gone.",
  "## Help along the way",
  "Morgan had help. Archbishop John Whitgift of Canterbury supported the work. Gabriel Goodman, the Welsh dean of Westminster, gave him a home in London while the Bible went through the press. Edmwnd Prys, archdeacon of Meirionnydd, and other scholars helped him. In the dedication to Queen Elizabeth, Morgan wrote that religion, if it were not taught in the mother tongue, would lie hidden and unknown.",
  "## After 1588",
  "Morgan became Bishop of Llandaf in 1595 and of St Asaph in 1601, and died in 1604. In 1620 his Bible was revised by Bishop Richard Parry and Dr John Davies of Mallwyd, and this became the Bible of the Welsh for three hundred years. In 1630 came Y Beibl Bach, the little Bible, small and cheap enough for families to buy for their own homes. From it the Welsh learned to read, in the circulating schools of Griffith Jones and the Sunday schools of Thomas Charles. Tŷ Mawr Wybrnant, where Morgan was born, is now cared for by the National Trust.",
 ],
 pCy=[
  "|| Argraffwyd | 1588, yn Llundain, gan ddirprwyon Christopher Barker",
  "|| Cyfieithwyd gan | William Morgan, ficer Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant",
  "|| O’r | Hebraeg a’r Groeg",
  "|| Gosodwyd | ym mhob eglwys blwyf yng Nghymru",
  "## Y llyfr a achubodd iaith",
  "Daeth Y Beibl Cyssegr-lan allan yn 1588, blwyddyn Armada Sbaen. Gwaith William Morgan ydoedd, a anwyd yn Nhŷ Mawr Wybrnant, yn y bryniau uwchben Penmachno, mab i ffermwr tenant, a astudiodd yng Nghaergrawnt a dod yn ficer Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant ym mryniau’r Berwyn. Yno, mewn plwyf gwledig, cyfieithodd y Beibl cyfan i’r Gymraeg o’r Hebraeg a’r Groeg, a diwygio Testament Newydd Salesbury hefyd.",
  "Cymraeg y beirdd oedd ei Gymraeg ef: cyfoethog, urddasol a chlir, iaith yr ysgolion barddol a gadwodd yr iaith yn bur am fil o flynyddoedd. Pan osodwyd ei Feibl ym mhob eglwys blwyf, clywid yr iaith honno bob Sul ym mhob cwr o Gymru. Rhoddodd i’r Cymry safon o Gymraeg ysgrifenedig y gallai pawb ei rhannu, gogledd a de, a dyna’r rheswm, cred llawer, fod Cymraeg yn cael ei siarad heddiw pan fo cynifer o ieithoedd eraill yr ynysoedd hyn wedi mynd.",
  "## Cymorth ar y ffordd",
  "Cafodd Morgan gymorth. Cefnogodd yr Archesgob John Whitgift o Gaergaint y gwaith. Rhoddodd Gabriel Goodman, deon Cymreig Westminster, gartref iddo yn Llundain tra âi’r Beibl drwy’r wasg. Bu Edmwnd Prys, archddiacon Meirionnydd, ac ysgolheigion eraill yn ei helpu. Yn y cyflwyniad i’r Frenhines Elisabeth, ysgrifennodd Morgan y byddai crefydd, oni ddysgid hi yn yr iaith frodorol, yn aros yn guddiedig ac yn anhysbys.",
  "## Wedi 1588",
  "Daeth Morgan yn Esgob Llandaf yn 1595 ac yn Esgob Llanelwy yn 1601, a bu farw yn 1604. Yn 1620 diwygiwyd ei Feibl gan yr Esgob Richard Parry a Dr John Davies o Fallwyd, a hwn fu Beibl y Cymry am dri chan mlynedd. Yn 1630 daeth Y Beibl Bach, yn ddigon bach a rhad i deuluoedd ei brynu i’w cartrefi eu hunain. Ohono y dysgodd y Cymry ddarllen, yn ysgolion cylchynol Griffith Jones ac ysgolion Sul Thomas Charles. Mae Tŷ Mawr Wybrnant, lle ganwyd Morgan, yng ngofal yr Ymddiriedolaeth Genedlaethol heddiw.",
 ])

PAGES["bardd-cwsc"] = dict(people=["ellis-wynne"],
 links=[("person-ellis-wynne.html", "Ellis Wynne", "Ellis Wynne"), ("literature.html#argraffu", "The printed book and the Bible", "Y llyfr printiedig a’r Beibl")],
 pEn=[
  "|| Printed | 1703, in London",
  "|| Written by | Ellis Wynne of Y Lasynys, near Harlech (1671 to 1734)",
  "|| Three visions | the Course of the World, Death, and Hell",
  "|| Famous for | one of the masterpieces of Welsh prose",
  "## A poet asleep on a mountain",
  "Gweledigaetheu y Bardd Cwsc, the Visions of the Sleeping Bard, begins on a summer evening on a mountain in Meirionnydd. The poet climbs to look out over the sea towards Ireland, falls asleep, and is carried off by an angel to see the world as it truly is. In three visions he is shown the City of Destruction, with all the follies and vices of the age, then the land of Death, and last the depths of Hell.",
  "## Its author",
  "Ellis Wynne was a gentleman and clergyman of Y Lasynys, a farmhouse on the shore near Harlech, who became rector of Llanfair, just inland. He took the idea of his book from the Spanish visions of Quevedo, which he had read in English, but he made it wholly Welsh. His sinners are the squires, lawyers, tavern keepers and fine ladies of Wales; his streets are the streets of Welsh towns, and his speech is the rich, quick, witty Welsh of Meirionnydd.",
  "## Why it is loved",
  "It has never been out of favour. It was printed again and again through the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, and generations of Welsh children learned their best Welsh from it. Writers still go back to it for the music of its sentences. Y Lasynys, Ellis Wynne’s home, has been restored and can be visited.",
 ],
 pCy=[
  "|| Argraffwyd | 1703, yn Llundain",
  "|| Ysgrifennwyd gan | Ellis Wynne o’r Lasynys, ger Harlech (1671 hyd 1734)",
  "|| Tair gweledigaeth | Cwrs y Byd, Angau, ac Uffern",
  "|| Enwog am | un o gampweithiau rhyddiaith Gymraeg",
  "## Bardd yn cysgu ar fynydd",
  "Mae Gweledigaetheu y Bardd Cwsc yn dechrau ar noson o haf ar fynydd ym Meirionnydd. Mae’r bardd yn dringo i edrych dros y môr tua Iwerddon, yn syrthio i gysgu, ac yn cael ei gludo gan angel i weld y byd fel y mae mewn gwirionedd. Mewn tair gweledigaeth dangosir iddo Ddinas Ddihenydd, gyda holl ffolinebau a beiau’r oes, yna gwlad Angau, ac yn olaf dyfnderoedd Uffern.",
  "## Ei awdur",
  "Bonheddwr a chlerigwr oedd Ellis Wynne o’r Lasynys, ffermdy ar y lan ger Harlech, a ddaeth yn rheithor Llanfair, gerllaw. Cymerodd syniad ei lyfr o weledigaethau Sbaenaidd Quevedo, a ddarllenodd yn Saesneg, ond gwnaeth ef yn gwbl Gymreig. Sgweiriaid, cyfreithwyr, tafarnwyr a boneddigesau Cymru yw ei bechaduriaid; strydoedd trefi Cymru yw ei strydoedd, a’i iaith yw Cymraeg cyfoethog, chwim a ffraeth Meirionnydd.",
  "## Pam mae’n annwyl",
  "Ni fu erioed allan o ffafr. Fe’i hargraffwyd dro ar ôl tro drwy’r ddeunawfed a’r bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg, a dysgodd cenedlaethau o blant Cymru eu Cymraeg gorau ohono. Mae llenorion yn dal i droi ato am gerddoriaeth ei frawddegau. Mae’r Lasynys, cartref Ellis Wynne, wedi’i adfer ac mae modd ymweld ag ef.",
 ])

PAGES["drych"] = dict(people=["theophilus-evans"],
 links=[("person-theophilus-evans.html", "Theophilus Evans", "Theophilus Evans"), ("story-brutus.html", "Brutus of Troy, father of the Britons", "Brutus o Gaerdroea, tad y Brythoniaid")],
 pEn=[
  "|| Printed | 1716; a new, fuller edition 1740",
  "|| Written by | Theophilus Evans (1693 to 1767), of Newcastle Emlyn and Llangamarch",
  "|| Tells | the story of the Britons, from the beginning to the age of the princes",
  "|| Famous for | the history the Welsh read at home for two hundred years",
  "## A mirror of the first ages",
  "Drych y Prif Oesoedd, the Mirror of the First Ages, is the story of the Welsh from the beginning, told by a young clergyman with fire in his heart. Theophilus Evans was born near Newcastle Emlyn in 1693 and wrote the first edition when he was only twenty-three. In 1740 he brought out a new edition, rewritten and enlarged, and it is that book which the Welsh came to love.",
  "## What it tells",
  "It tells of the Britons, descended from Gomer, grandson of Noah, and from Brutus of Troy; of their kings and their wars against the Romans, of Caradog and Buddug; of the coming of Christianity to the Britons long before Augustine came to the English; of Gwrtheyrn and the treachery of the long knives; and of Arthur and the long fight for the Island of Britain. Evans wrote to make his people proud of their history and their faith, and he did.",
  "## A book for every hearth",
  "The Drych was printed again and again and read aloud in Welsh homes for two hundred years. O. M. Edwards printed it once more at the end of the nineteenth century in his series of Welsh classics, and it is still counted among the finest Welsh prose. Theophilus Evans is remembered for another gift too: as vicar of Llangamarch, he found the healing waters of Llanwrtyd in 1732, and so began Llanwrtyd Wells.",
 ],
 pCy=[
  "|| Argraffwyd | 1716; argraffiad newydd, llawnach 1740",
  "|| Ysgrifennwyd gan | Theophilus Evans (1693 hyd 1767), o Gastellnewydd Emlyn a Llangamarch",
  "|| Yn adrodd | stori’r Brythoniaid, o’r dechrau hyd oes y tywysogion",
  "|| Enwog am | yr hanes a ddarllenai’r Cymry gartref am ddau gan mlynedd",
  "## Drych y prif oesoedd",
  "Drych y Prif Oesoedd yw stori’r Cymry o’r dechrau, wedi’i hadrodd gan glerigwr ifanc â thân yn ei galon. Ganwyd Theophilus Evans ger Castellnewydd Emlyn yn 1693 ac ysgrifennodd yr argraffiad cyntaf pan nad oedd ond tair ar hugain oed. Yn 1740 cyhoeddodd argraffiad newydd, wedi’i ailysgrifennu a’i helaethu, a’r llyfr hwnnw a ddaeth y Cymry i’w garu.",
  "## Beth mae’n ei adrodd",
  "Mae’n sôn am y Brythoniaid, disgynyddion Gomer, ŵyr Noa, a Brutus o Gaerdroea; am eu brenhinoedd a’u rhyfeloedd yn erbyn y Rhufeiniaid, am Garadog a Buddug; am ddyfodiad Cristnogaeth at y Brythoniaid ymhell cyn i Awstin ddod at y Saeson; am Wrtheyrn a brad y cyllyll hirion; ac am Arthur a’r frwydr hir dros Ynys Prydain. Ysgrifennodd Evans i wneud ei bobl yn falch o’u hanes a’u ffydd, a llwyddodd.",
  "## Llyfr i bob aelwyd",
  "Argraffwyd y Drych dro ar ôl tro a’i ddarllen yn uchel ar aelwydydd Cymru am ddau gan mlynedd. Argraffodd O. M. Edwards ef unwaith eto ar ddiwedd y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg yn ei gyfres o glasuron Cymraeg, ac fe’i cyfrifir o hyd ymhlith y rhyddiaith Gymraeg orau. Cofir Theophilus Evans am rodd arall hefyd: fel ficer Llangamarch, darganfu ddyfroedd iachusol Llanwrtyd yn 1732, a dyna ddechrau Llanwrtyd.",
 ])

PAGES["myvyrian"] = dict(people=["owain-myfyr", "iolo-morganwg", "william-owen-pughe"],
 links=[("story-iolo.html", "Iolo Morganwg", "Iolo Morganwg"), ("person-owain-myfyr.html", "Owain Myfyr", "Owain Myfyr"), ("triads.html", "The Welsh Triads", "Trioedd Ynys Prydain")],
 pEn=[
  "|| Printed | London: volumes I and II in 1801, volume III in 1807",
  "|| Made by | Owain Myfyr, Iolo Morganwg and William Owen Pughe",
  "|| Paid for by | Owain Myfyr, a London furrier from Llanfihangel Glyn Myfyr",
  "|| Second edition | Denbigh, 1870, by Thomas Gee",
  "## The treasures of Wales in print",
  "Until 1801 the old poetry and prose of Wales lay in manuscripts, scattered in country houses and libraries, where few could read them. The Myvyrian Archaiology of Wales put them into print for the first time, in three great volumes.",
  "It was the dream of Owen Jones, known as Owain Myfyr after his home parish of Llanfihangel Glyn Myfyr in Denbighshire. He had gone to London as a young man and grown rich in the fur trade, and he spent his fortune on the literature of his people. He founded the Gwyneddigion Society for the Welsh of London, paid for the printing of the poems of Dafydd ap Gwilym in 1789, and then paid for the Archaiology.",
  "## Three makers",
  "With him worked two of the most remarkable Welshmen of their age. Iolo Morganwg, the stonemason poet of Glamorgan, who knew more of the old learning of Wales than any man alive, travelled the country copying manuscripts. William Owen Pughe, the maker of the great Welsh dictionary, edited and arranged. Together they printed the poems of Taliesin and Aneirin, the Gododdin, the Poets of the Princes, the chronicles, the Bruts, the laws and the triads.",
  "## The triads of the third series",
  "The Archaiology also printed new treasures from the manuscripts of Glamorgan, in Iolo’s own account: the chronicles of Aberpergwm and Ieuan Brechfa, and a great third series of triads, which tell of Hu Gadarn who led the Cymry to the Island of Britain, and of the wisdom of the bards. They were read and loved all over Wales. In 1870 Thomas Gee of Denbigh printed the whole work again in one volume, and it remained the great storehouse of Welsh literature for generations.",
 ],
 pCy=[
  "|| Argraffwyd | Llundain: cyfrolau I a II yn 1801, cyfrol III yn 1807",
  "|| Gwnaed gan | Owain Myfyr, Iolo Morganwg a William Owen Pughe",
  "|| Talwyd gan | Owain Myfyr, crwynwr yn Llundain o Lanfihangel Glyn Myfyr",
  "|| Ail argraffiad | Dinbych, 1870, gan Thomas Gee",
  "## Trysorau Cymru mewn print",
  "Hyd 1801 gorweddai hen farddoniaeth a rhyddiaith Cymru mewn llawysgrifau, ar chwâl mewn plastai a llyfrgelloedd, lle na allai ond ychydig eu darllen. Rhoddodd y Myvyrian Archaiology of Wales hwy mewn print am y tro cyntaf, mewn tair cyfrol fawr.",
  "Breuddwyd Owen Jones ydoedd, a adwaenid fel Owain Myfyr ar ôl ei blwyf genedigol, Llanfihangel Glyn Myfyr yn Sir Ddinbych. Aethai i Lundain yn llanc a gwneud ei ffortiwn yn y fasnach grwyn, a gwariodd ei ffortiwn ar lenyddiaeth ei bobl. Sefydlodd Gymdeithas y Gwyneddigion i Gymry Llundain, talodd am argraffu cerddi Dafydd ap Gwilym yn 1789, ac yna talodd am yr Archaiology.",
  "## Tri gwneuthurwr",
  "Gydag ef gweithiai dau o Gymry mwyaf rhyfeddol eu hoes. Teithiodd Iolo Morganwg, bardd a saer maen o Forgannwg, a wyddai fwy o hen ddysg Cymru nag unrhyw ddyn byw, drwy’r wlad yn copïo llawysgrifau. Golygodd a threfnodd William Owen Pughe, gwneuthurwr y geiriadur Cymraeg mawr. Gyda’i gilydd argraffasant gerddi Taliesin ac Aneirin, y Gododdin, Beirdd y Tywysogion, y croniclau, y Brutiau, y cyfreithiau a’r trioedd.",
  "## Trioedd y drydedd gyfres",
  "Argraffodd yr Archaiology hefyd drysorau newydd o lawysgrifau Morgannwg, yn ôl tystiolaeth Iolo ei hun: croniclau Aberpergwm a Ieuan Brechfa, a thrydedd gyfres fawr o drioedd, sy’n sôn am Hu Gadarn a arweiniodd y Cymry i Ynys Prydain, ac am ddoethineb y beirdd. Fe’u darllenwyd a’u caru ledled Cymru. Yn 1870 argraffodd Thomas Gee o Ddinbych y gwaith cyfan eto mewn un gyfrol, a bu’n stordy mawr llenyddiaeth Gymraeg am genedlaethau.",
 ])
